ation you'll see  harsh flavors on the screen where  um i don't have time to talk about that  today  but it's another thing that we're really  interested in and each of these  accessions is being crossed to two  popular inbreds which can be thought of  as either biological replicates or kind  of like a tester  um and then these are progressed to f  the f2 generation  so the phenotypic selection is serving  as kind of our current standard approach  using data from hancock wisconsin  a couple years ago the first flavor of  the  genomic selection we're determining the  genomic reading values  strategy in which we're using available  reading program data to calculate  reading values for untested accessions  i know i'm kind of racing through these  i'm more than happy to talk more  in detail about these strategies later  in the chat  and then finally we are also working  with the genomic training population  strategy  and which were in which we identified a  genetically representative training  population  which we then phenotyped in our target  environment this one maximized the  relationship between the  test and the training population which  um  can dramatically increase predictive  accuracy of models but it did require an  extra year of phenotyping before  breeding could begin  next i'm going to talk about just some  of the results we've been able to  achieve so far  obviously the main thrust of this work  is going to be  the comparison of breeding populations  and  i'm really glad to report we just got  approval to actually have a field season  this year which wasn't  clear a few weeks ago so while we have  some pilot data we're going to be able  to dramatically  increase that next year by doing a  replicated trial of these breeding  populations and measuring them for the  traits we're interested in  as we were developing our genomic  training population strategy we also  uh were able to test different  parameters of the training population  uh using cross validation so what we  looked at was both composition and also  relative size  compared to the total collection which  is what you see here  and then finally and this has become  somewhat of a pet project of mine  uh we're beginning to analyze intra  accession diversity  so observationally we note a fair degree  of heterogeneity within a given  accession  and i think moving forward the success  of this type of approach is going to  rest not only on improving strategies to  exploit variation between accessions but  also  methods to quantify  and track diversity within accessions as  well  so in closing  we believe that genomic assisted  selection can help small programs  use genetic resources more effectively  but what we're finding is that high  prediction accuracy  seems to require large training  populations  [Music]  but even moderate accuracy still may be  useful to programs who really just need  to reduce the number of accessions that  they're screening  and our future will work we'll  compare phenotypic and genotypic  selection in more detail  and we'll continue to track enter  accession diversity  with that um i'd like to thank people  who have  really been about invaluable to this  work um and i can take any questions  thank you  great thank you so much ko um i guess  we'll be  taking questions there's nothing in the  chat  so if you want to go ahead and raise  your hand and you can ask a question  uh looks like hannah uh hannah jeffrey  has a question  um you can go ahead and meet yourself  anna  thank you my question is about how  large do you think your training  populations will need to be in the  future  and what kind of statistical modeling  have you  implemented to try to predict what size  it will have to be  um  sure thank you for that question um  can you still hear me i'm not sure what  i'm looking at right now  um okay thank you yeah so  we saw a pretty dramatic improvement in  prediction accuracy  when we increased the size of our  training populations 